mkntfs, ntfsfix and ntfsinfo pass getopt_long() an option string that
starts with '-', the request to have every operand returned in order as
option 1, and pick up the device only there.  The LikeOS getopt() follows
POSIX instead: it stops at the first operand.  Without this change none of
the three would find its device ("You must specify a device").

Each tool now also takes the operands that are left after the option loop,
in the order and with the meaning option 1 gives them (mkntfs: device, then
number of sectors; ntfsfix and ntfsinfo: the device).  Where getopt()
returns operands in order nothing is left after the loop and nothing
changes.  ntfslabel already does this.

